      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="Eclipse Dataspace Components" id="id-5245ef9844e44f1686ee2058743e0b41">
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EclipseDataspaceComponentsAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EclipseDataspaceComponentsAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:EclipseDataspaceComponentsAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Eclipse Dataspace Components Assessment"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Eclipse Dataspace Components (EDC) is an open-source project under the Eclipse Foundation that provides a platform for developing and managing IoT applications. It offers a set of modular components that can be used to collect, process, and distribute data from various devices and sensors. EDC also supports edge computing, allowing data processing to occur closer to the source, reducing latency and bandwidth usage. It is designed to be flexible and scalable, supporting a wide range of IoT use cases across different industries."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="1. Eclipse Dataspace Connector (EDC): EDC is a software framework that enables secure and reliable data exchange between different systems. It provides a set of APIs and services that allow developers to build applications that can connect, interact, and exchange data with other systems in a secure and reliable manner.&#xA;&#xA;2. Eclipse Dataspace Runtime: This is a runtime environment that supports the execution of applications built using the Eclipse Dataspace Connector. It provides a set of services and capabilities that enable the secure and reliable execution of these applications.&#xA;&#xA;3. Eclipse Dataspace SDK: This is a software development kit that provides a set of tools and libraries that developers can use to build applications that use the Eclipse Dataspace Connector. It includes APIs, documentation, sample code, and other resources that make it easier for developers to build these applications.&#xA;&#xA;4. Eclipse Dataspace Management Console: This is a web-based interface that allows administrators to manage and monitor the operation of the Eclipse Dataspace Connector and the applications that use it. It provides capabilities for configuring the connector, monitoring its operation, and troubleshooting any issues that may arise.&#xA;&#xA;5. Eclipse Dataspace Security Services: These are a set of services that provide security capabilities for the Eclipse Dataspace Connector and the applications that use it. They include services for authentication, authorization, encryption, and other security-related functions."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="Eclipse Dataspace Components (EDC) is an open-source project under the Eclipse Foundation that aims to provide a set of reusable components for building data-centric applications. These components are designed to help developers manage and manipulate data in a more efficient and effective way.&#xA;&#xA;EDC is built on the Eclipse Modeling Framework (EMF), which is a modeling framework and code generation facility for building tools and other applications based on a structured data model. This allows EDC to provide a consistent and unified approach to data management, making it easier for developers to work with data in their applications.&#xA;&#xA;The components provided by EDC cover a wide range of data management tasks. These include components for data access, data transformation, data validation, and data visualization. Each component is designed to be reusable and customizable, allowing developers to tailor them to their specific needs.&#xA;&#xA;Data Access Components are used for reading and writing data from various sources, such as databases, files, and web services. They provide a unified API for data access, abstracting the underlying data source and allowing developers to work with data in a consistent way.&#xA;&#xA;Data Transformation Components are used for converting data from one format to another. They support a wide range of data formats and provide a flexible and powerful transformation engine.&#xA;&#xA;Data Validation Components are used for checking the integrity and consistency of data. They provide a set of validation rules that can be applied to data to ensure it meets certain criteria.&#xA;&#xA;Data Visualization Components are used for presenting data in a visual format. They support a variety of visualization techniques, including charts, graphs, and tables.&#xA;&#xA;In addition to these components, EDC also provides a set of utilities for common data management tasks, such as data filtering, sorting, and paging.&#xA;&#xA;Overall, Eclipse Dataspace Components is a comprehensive solution for data management in application development. It provides a set of powerful and flexible components that can significantly simplify the task of working with data, allowing developers to focus more on the business logic of their applications."/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base" id="id-53b1b5c53de74879b58f13119ed49403">
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/JRCDataSpacesKnowledgeBaseAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/JRCDataSpacesKnowledgeBaseAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:JRCDataSpacesKnowledgeBaseAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base Assessment"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="The JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base is a digital platform developed by the Joint Research Centre (JRC) of the European Commission. It serves as a comprehensive resource for information related to data spaces, which are virtual environments where data is exchanged and shared. The Knowledge Base includes a wide range of materials such as reports, case studies, technical guides, and policy documents. It aims to support the development and implementation of data spaces by providing relevant and up-to-date information to stakeholders, including policymakers, researchers, and data professionals."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="The JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base is a comprehensive resource that provides access to various datasets and information related to different fields of study. Here are some examples:&#xA;&#xA;1. Agriculture and Rural Development: This data space includes information about crop yield, livestock production, agricultural practices, and rural development policies across different regions.&#xA;&#xA;2. Climate Change: This data space provides datasets related to climate change indicators, greenhouse gas emissions, climate models, and impacts of climate change on various sectors.&#xA;&#xA;3. Energy: This data space includes data on energy production, consumption, energy efficiency, renewable energy sources, and energy policies.&#xA;&#xA;4. Health and Food Safety: This data space provides information on food safety standards, health indicators, disease outbreaks, and health policies.&#xA;&#xA;5. Migration and Demography: This data space includes data on migration flows, population demographics, social integration, and policies related to migration and demography.&#xA;&#xA;6. Transport and Mobility: This data space provides datasets on transport networks, mobility patterns, transport emissions, and transport policies.&#xA;&#xA;7. Digital Economy: This data space includes information on digital technologies, internet usage, e-commerce, digital skills, and digital policies.&#xA;&#xA;8. Environment and Sustainability: This data space provides data on environmental indicators, biodiversity, waste management, water resources, and sustainability policies.&#xA;&#xA;9. Education and Culture: This data space includes data on education systems, cultural heritage, language diversity, and policies related to education and culture.&#xA;&#xA;10. Security and Justice: This data space provides information on crime rates, justice systems, security threats, and policies related to security and justice."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base is a comprehensive online resource developed by the Joint Research Centre (JRC) of the European Commission. It is designed to provide a wide range of information and resources related to data spaces, which are virtual environments where organizations can share, exchange, and work with data.&#xA;&#xA;The Knowledge Base is a part of the JRC's broader initiative to support the development and implementation of data spaces in Europe, in line with the European strategy for data. It aims to facilitate the understanding, design, and operation of data spaces by providing a variety of resources, including technical guides, policy documents, case studies, and research papers.&#xA;&#xA;The JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base covers a wide range of topics related to data spaces, including data governance, data interoperability, data infrastructure, and data sharing. It also provides information on various aspects of data spaces, such as their architecture, legal and ethical considerations, and potential applications in different sectors.&#xA;&#xA;The Knowledge Base is intended for a wide range of users, including policymakers, data professionals, researchers, and businesses. It aims to support these users in making informed decisions about the design and implementation of data spaces, and to promote the sharing and reuse of data in a secure and trustworthy manner.&#xA;&#xA;In addition to providing information and resources, the JRC Data Spaces Knowledge Base also serves as a platform for dialogue and collaboration. It encourages users to contribute their own knowledge and experiences, and to engage in discussions and collaborations with other users. This collaborative approach is intended to foster a vibrant and dynamic community of practice around data spaces, and to support the ongoing development and evolution of data spaces in Europe."/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="International Data Spaces Reference Architecture Model (IDS-RAM)" id="id-6ef397044436456aa4f6504ba511311e">
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/InternationalDataSpacesReferenceArchitectureModelAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/InternationalDataSpacesReferenceArchitectureModelAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:InternationalDataSpacesReferenceArchitectureModelAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="International Data Spaces Reference Architecture Model (IDS-RAM) Assessment"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="The International Data Spaces Reference Architecture Model (IDS-RAM) is a comprehensive framework designed to facilitate secure and standardized data exchange between different organizations. It provides a set of guidelines and specifications for creating a trusted data sharing environment, ensuring data sovereignty and data usage control. The IDS-RAM encompasses various components including connectors, app store, clearing house, and vocabulary, among others, which work together to enable interoperability, data security, and privacy. It is developed and maintained by the International Data Spaces Association, a European non-profit organization."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="The International Data Spaces Reference Architecture Model (IDS-RAM) is a framework that provides guidelines for creating secure and standardized data spaces. It is designed to facilitate data exchange and ensure data sovereignty in business ecosystems. Here are some examples of how IDS-RAM can be applied:&#xA;&#xA;1. Supply Chain Management: IDS-RAM can be used to create a secure data space for sharing information between suppliers, manufacturers, and retailers. This can help to improve efficiency, reduce costs, and enhance transparency in the supply chain.&#xA;&#xA;2. Healthcare: In the healthcare sector, IDS-RAM can be used to create a secure data space for sharing patient data between healthcare providers, insurance companies, and research institutions. This can help to improve patient care, streamline administrative processes, and facilitate medical research.&#xA;&#xA;3. Smart Cities: IDS-RAM can be used to create a secure data space for sharing data between various stakeholders in a smart city, such as government agencies, utility companies, and transportation providers. This can help to improve urban planning, enhance public services, and promote sustainability.&#xA;&#xA;4. Agriculture: In the agricultural sector, IDS-RAM can be used to create a secure data space for sharing data between farmers, suppliers, and agricultural research institutions. This can help to improve crop yield, reduce waste, and promote sustainable farming practices.&#xA;&#xA;5. Automotive Industry: IDS-RAM can be used to create a secure data space for sharing data between car manufacturers, suppliers, and service providers. This can help to improve vehicle design, enhance safety features, and streamline maintenance processes.&#xA;&#xA;6. Energy Sector: IDS-RAM can be used to create a secure data space for sharing data between energy producers, distributors, and consumers. This can help to improve energy efficiency, reduce costs, and promote renewable energy sources."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The International Data Spaces Reference Architecture Model (IDS-RAM) is a comprehensive framework that outlines the principles and mechanisms for a secure and standardized data exchange between different stakeholders in a digital ecosystem. It is developed by the International Data Spaces Association (IDSA), a European initiative aiming to facilitate the sovereign, self-determined exchange and usage of industrial data.&#xA;&#xA;The IDS-RAM is designed to ensure data sovereignty for all participants, meaning that data providers maintain full control over their data at all times, deciding who can access it, for what purpose, and under what conditions. It provides a blueprint for creating a decentralized architecture where data can be exchanged in a trusted, secure, and interoperable manner.&#xA;&#xA;The model is based on several key components:&#xA;&#xA;1. IDS Connectors: These are the main components of the IDS architecture. They act as data processors and data providers, ensuring secure data exchange between participants.&#xA;&#xA;2. IDS Broker: This component helps participants find each other by providing a registry of available data sources and services.&#xA;&#xA;3. IDS Clearing House: This component records transactions in a verifiable and tamper-proof manner, ensuring transparency and traceability.&#xA;&#xA;4. IDS App Store: This is a platform where participants can offer and obtain data-driven applications and services.&#xA;&#xA;5. IDS Vocabulary: This provides a common language for all participants, ensuring interoperability.&#xA;&#xA;6. IDS Certification: This ensures that all components and participants comply with IDS standards and principles.&#xA;&#xA;The IDS-RAM also includes various layers such as the business layer, the functional layer, the process layer, the system layer, and the infrastructure layer, each of which plays a crucial role in the overall architecture.&#xA;&#xA;The IDS-RAM is not a software or a product, but a set of guidelines and standards that organizations can adopt to create a secure and trusted data exchange environment. It is technology-neutral and can be implemented using various technologies such as cloud computing, edge computing, blockchain, and others. It is designed to be flexible and adaptable to different use cases and sectors, from manufacturing and logistics to healthcare and agriculture."/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="Data Spaces Business Alliance" id="id-aa057c72367e4f488d49df556af255a1">
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/DataSpacesBusinessAllianceAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/DataSpacesBusinessAllianceAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:DataSpacesBusinessAllianceAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Data Spaces Business Alliance Assessment"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="The Data Spaces Business Alliance is a global initiative launched by several leading technology companies, including Atos, Dell Technologies, Google Cloud, and IBM. The alliance aims to develop a set of standards, protocols, and best practices for data sharing and collaboration across industries. The goal is to create secure and trusted data spaces that enable businesses to share and leverage data in a privacy-compliant manner, fostering innovation and driving digital transformation."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="1. Snowflake and Databricks: Snowflake, a cloud-based data warehousing startup, and Databricks, a big data analytics service, have formed a business alliance to provide a unified, open, and dynamic data ecosystem. This alliance allows customers to run Databricks' analytics platform on Snowflake's data cloud, enabling them to easily unify, analyze, and extract valuable insights from their data.&#xA;&#xA;2. Google Cloud and SAP: Google Cloud and SAP have formed a business alliance to help customers execute business transformations, migrate critical business systems to the cloud, and augment existing business systems with Google Cloud capabilities in artificial intelligence and machine learning.&#xA;&#xA;3. Microsoft and Adobe: Microsoft and Adobe have formed a strategic partnership to redefine customer experiences. The partnership makes Adobe Marketing Cloud the preferred marketing service for Microsoft Dynamics 365 Enterprise edition.&#xA;&#xA;4. IBM and Salesforce: IBM and Salesforce have partnered to deliver joint solutions designed to leverage artificial intelligence and enable companies to make smarter decisions, faster. The partnership brings together IBM Watson and Salesforce Einstein to enable an entirely new level of intelligent customer engagement across sales, service, marketing, commerce and more.&#xA;&#xA;5. Amazon Web Services and Tableau: Tableau, a leading data visualization tool, has partnered with Amazon Web Services to provide a comprehensive cloud-based analytics solution. This partnership allows users to connect, visualize, and share data with a seamless experience from the source in AWS to the visualization in Tableau.&#xA;&#xA;6. Oracle and VMware: Oracle and VMware have formed a strategic partnership to support customers’ hybrid cloud strategies and enable them to leverage their existing software investments while enjoying new technology advancements. This partnership allows customers to run, manage, connect, and secure applications across VMware environments and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.&#xA;&#xA;7. Cisco and NetApp: Cisco and NetApp have formed a business alliance to deliver a portfolio of integrated solutions designed to simplify and modernize IT and deliver enhanced data management capabilities. The partnership combines the innovation of Cisco UCS Integrated Infrastructure with the simplicity of NetApp data services."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The Data Spaces Business Alliance is a global initiative established by a group of leading technology companies, including Atos, Dell Technologies, Google Cloud, IBM, Intel, Microsoft, and SAP. The alliance aims to create a trusted and secure data sharing environment that enables businesses to collaborate and innovate through shared data spaces. &#xA;&#xA;The Data Spaces Business Alliance is built on the principle of data sovereignty, which means that organizations maintain control over their data, even when it is shared or combined with data from other organizations. This is achieved through the use of secure and standardized technologies that ensure data privacy and security.&#xA;&#xA;The alliance's goal is to create a global standard for data spaces, which are virtual environments where organizations can share and collaborate on data. These data spaces are designed to be interoperable, meaning that they can work together seamlessly, regardless of the technology or platform used.&#xA;&#xA;The Data Spaces Business Alliance also aims to foster a vibrant ecosystem of technology providers, data providers, and data users. This ecosystem will support the development and adoption of data spaces, and will provide businesses with the tools and resources they need to leverage shared data for innovation and growth.&#xA;&#xA;The alliance believes that by enabling secure and trusted data sharing, it can help businesses unlock new opportunities, drive innovation, and create value. This could include developing new products and services, improving operational efficiency, or gaining insights for decision-making.&#xA;&#xA;In summary, the Data Spaces Business Alliance is a global initiative that aims to create a secure and trusted environment for data sharing, based on the principle of data sovereignty. It seeks to establish a global standard for data spaces, foster a vibrant ecosystem of stakeholders, and help businesses unlock the value of shared data."/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="FIWARE for Data Spaces" id="id-b873e20ecd834c4a8c1ac9cd6e8aebef">
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/FIWAREForDataSpacesAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/FIWAREForDataSpacesAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:FIWAREForDataSpacesAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="FIWARE For Data Spaces Assessment"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="FIWARE for Data Spaces is a platform that enables the creation and management of data spaces, which are secure and trusted environments where organizations can share and exchange data. It provides a set of standardized components and APIs to facilitate data sharing and interoperability, while ensuring data sovereignty and compliance with data protection regulations. This platform supports a variety of data spaces, including personal, business, and public data spaces, and can be used in various sectors such as agriculture, smart cities, and industry 4.0."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="FIWARE is an open-source platform that provides a simple yet powerful set of APIs (Application Programming Interfaces) that ease the development of smart applications in multiple vertical sectors. It is used to create data spaces that can be utilized in various sectors. Here are some examples:&#xA;&#xA;1. Smart Cities: FIWARE can be used to create a data space for a smart city, where data from various sources like traffic sensors, weather stations, and city databases can be integrated and analyzed. This can help in better urban planning, traffic management, and environmental monitoring.&#xA;&#xA;2. Agriculture: In the agriculture sector, FIWARE can be used to create a data space that integrates data from various sources like soil sensors, weather stations, and satellite imagery. This can help farmers in making informed decisions about irrigation, fertilization, and crop health monitoring.&#xA;&#xA;3. Healthcare: FIWARE can be used to create a data space in the healthcare sector, integrating data from various sources like patient records, medical devices, and health apps. This can help in better patient care, disease prediction, and health monitoring.&#xA;&#xA;4. Industry 4.0: In the industrial sector, FIWARE can be used to create a data space that integrates data from various sources like machine sensors, production data, and supply chain data. This can help in predictive maintenance, production optimization, and supply chain management.&#xA;&#xA;5. Energy: FIWARE can be used to create a data space in the energy sector, integrating data from various sources like smart meters, grid data, and weather data. This can help in energy management, demand prediction, and grid optimization.&#xA;&#xA;6. Retail: In the retail sector, FIWARE can be used to create a data space that integrates data from various sources like sales data, customer data, and inventory data. This can help in sales prediction, customer behavior analysis, and inventory management."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="FIWARE for Data Spaces is a comprehensive solution designed to facilitate the creation, management, and operation of data spaces. It is a part of the FIWARE platform, an open-source initiative that aims to provide a simple yet powerful set of APIs (Application Programming Interfaces) that ease the development of smart applications in various domains.&#xA;&#xA;FIWARE for Data Spaces is built around the concept of data spaces, which are virtual environments where organizations can share and exchange data in a secure, controlled, and standardized manner. These data spaces can be used to create data marketplaces, where data providers and consumers can interact and conduct data transactions.&#xA;&#xA;The solution provides a wide range of features and capabilities to support the entire lifecycle of data spaces. This includes data publication, discovery, access control, data usage tracking, and monetization. It also supports various data formats and protocols, ensuring interoperability and flexibility.&#xA;&#xA;FIWARE for Data Spaces leverages advanced technologies such as big data, cloud computing, and artificial intelligence to provide robust, scalable, and intelligent data spaces. It also incorporates strong security measures to protect data privacy and integrity.&#xA;&#xA;The solution is designed with a modular and extensible architecture, allowing organizations to customize and extend its functionalities according to their specific needs. It also provides various tools and APIs to facilitate the integration with existing systems and the development of new applications.&#xA;&#xA;In summary, FIWARE for Data Spaces is a comprehensive and flexible solution that enables organizations to create, manage, and operate data spaces, facilitating secure and controlled data sharing and exchange. It provides a wide range of features and capabilities, leverages advanced technologies, and offers strong security measures, making it a suitable solution for various data-related needs and applications."/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="Key Structural IoP Enablers" id="id-da6af17c1a384ccfa83846d5d00a6091">
      <property key="dct:type" value="eira:KeyStructuralIoPEnablersAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2024-01-28"/>
      <property key="eira:synonym"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Key Structural IoP Enablers ABB is a Grouping enabling information interpretation to share and/or reuse of organizational resources of digital public services"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="ISA2-EIF"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference" value="https://ec.europa.eu/isa2/eif_en/"/>
      <property key="skos:example"/>
      <property key="eira:iopSaliency"/>
      <property key="skos:note"/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/KeyStructuralIoPEnablersAssessment"/>
      <property key="eira:iopDimension" value="Structural IoP"/>
      <property key="eira:viewpoint" value="Key Interoperability Enablers viewpoint"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/KeyStructuralIoPEnablersAssessment"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="Gaia-X Data Spaces Community" id="id-dd1863994f1744819b1bbde8e986239c">
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/Gaia-XDataSpacesCommunityAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/Gaia-XDataSpacesCommunityAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:Gaia-XDataSpacesCommunityAssessment"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Gaia-X Data Spaces Community Assessment"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="The Gaia-X Data Spaces Community is a collaborative initiative that aims to create a secure and trustworthy data infrastructure in Europe. It brings together various stakeholders, including businesses, research institutions, and public entities, to develop and implement standards for data sharing and usage. The community focuses on creating 'data spaces' - secure virtual environments where data can be shared and analyzed while ensuring privacy and compliance with European regulations. These data spaces are designed to foster innovation, enhance competitiveness, and support the digital sovereignty of Europe."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="The Gaia-X Data Spaces Community is a collaborative initiative aimed at creating a secure and trustworthy data infrastructure in Europe. Here are some examples of the community:&#xA;&#xA;1. Automotive Data Space: This community focuses on the automotive industry, working on solutions for secure data exchange between manufacturers, suppliers, and service providers. They might work on projects like connected cars, autonomous driving, and predictive maintenance.&#xA;&#xA;2. Health Data Space: This community works on solutions for secure and efficient data exchange in the healthcare sector. They might work on projects like electronic health records, telemedicine, and personalized medicine.&#xA;&#xA;3. Agriculture Data Space: This community focuses on the agricultural sector, working on solutions for secure data exchange between farmers, suppliers, and researchers. They might work on projects like precision farming, crop yield prediction, and sustainable farming practices.&#xA;&#xA;4. Energy Data Space: This community works on solutions for secure and efficient data exchange in the energy sector. They might work on projects like smart grids, renewable energy forecasting, and energy efficiency.&#xA;&#xA;5. Public Sector Data Space: This community focuses on the public sector, working on solutions for secure data exchange between government agencies, citizens, and businesses. They might work on projects like e-government, open data, and digital public services.&#xA;&#xA;6. Financial Services Data Space: This community works on solutions for secure and efficient data exchange in the financial services sector. They might work on projects like open banking, fraud detection, and risk management.&#xA;&#xA;7. Manufacturing Data Space: This community focuses on the manufacturing sector, working on solutions for secure data exchange between manufacturers, suppliers, and customers. They might work on projects like Industry 4.0, predictive maintenance, and supply chain optimization."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The Gaia-X Data Spaces Community is a collaborative initiative that aims to create a secure, trustworthy, and federated data infrastructure in Europe. This community is part of the larger Gaia-X project, which is a European endeavor to establish a competitive, secure, and trustworthy data infrastructure for Europe.&#xA;&#xA;The Gaia-X Data Spaces Community is composed of various stakeholders, including businesses, research institutions, public sector organizations, and other interested parties. These stakeholders work together to define the requirements, standards, and rules for data spaces, which are virtual environments where data can be shared and exchanged in a secure and controlled manner.&#xA;&#xA;The community's main goal is to create a framework for data spaces that ensures data sovereignty and data availability. Data sovereignty refers to the idea that data is subject to the laws and governance structures of the country in which it is located. Data availability, on the other hand, refers to the ability to access and use data when and where it is needed.&#xA;&#xA;The Gaia-X Data Spaces Community is organized into several working groups, each focusing on a specific aspect of data spaces. These working groups include the Data Spaces Architecture and Standards Working Group, the Data Spaces Use Cases and Requirements Working Group, and the Data Spaces Governance and Certification Working Group.&#xA;&#xA;The community also collaborates with other Gaia-X communities and working groups, as well as with external organizations and initiatives. This collaboration is crucial for ensuring that the data spaces framework is compatible with other components of the Gaia-X infrastructure and with the broader data ecosystem.&#xA;&#xA;In summary, the Gaia-X Data Spaces Community is a collaborative initiative that aims to create a secure, trustworthy, and federated data infrastructure in Europe. It brings together various stakeholders to define the requirements, standards, and rules for data spaces, with the goal of ensuring data sovereignty and data availability."/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="N/A"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Assessment" name="Key Behavioural IoP Enablers" id="id-fcf62d83ba2249608d415d153ae55fed">
