    <element xsi:type="archimate:Grouping" name="Health Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (EBI)" id="id-f7e69b424cff47c79be758f57b4a3e22">
      <documentation>Definition: Health Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (EBI) is a grouping that covers the legal and regulatory frameworks that guide the exchange and integration of health-related behavioural data across different health systems, platforms, and applications. It encompasses laws, policies, and standards that ensure seamless and secure sharing of behavioural health data to enhance patient care, improve health outcomes, and promote health research.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Additional information: Health Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (EBI) is crucial in the modern healthcare landscape where digital health technologies are increasingly being used to collect, analyze, and share health-related behavioural data. This legislation ensures that these technologies can interoperate, i.e., exchange and make use of the data, in a manner that is secure, efficient, and beneficial to patients, healthcare providers, and researchers. It covers aspects such as data privacy and security, data ownership, data standardization, and data sharing agreements. It also addresses the ethical and legal issues related to the use of behavioural health data.

Example: Examples of application of Health Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (EBI) include the implementation of data sharing agreements between different health systems to facilitate the exchange of behavioural health data, the development of standards for the collection and analysis of such data, and the enforcement of data privacy and security laws to protect patient information.

LOST view: LV-Architecture Principles

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/HealthLegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping

